    "whyItMatters": "An incorrect answer does not reveal its own cause. The learner may lack the concept, confuse two similar ideas, misread the task, select a later intervention before an earlier priority, or rush through a known rule. Each cause requires a different correction.",
    "learningObjectives": [
      "Classify common error types.",
      "Use an error log without copying entire questions.",
      "Distinguish topic weakness from reasoning-pattern weakness.",
      "Decide when a topic should remain a priority, move to developing, or enter maintenance."
    ],
    "coreExplanation": [
      "## The six error types",
      "### 1. Knowledge gap",
      "The learner did not know the required concept.",
      "**Example:** The learner cannot identify the purpose of a norm-referenced test.",
      "**Action:** Complete or revisit the foundational lesson, retrieve the concept, and answer targeted items.",
      "### 2. Concept confusion",
      "The learner knows both terms but cannot distinguish them.",
      "**Example:** Confusing random selection with random assignment.",
      "**Action:** Create a comparison rule and practice near-neighbor items.",
      "### 3. Stem or task error",
      "The learner answers a different question from the one asked.",
      "**Example:** The stem asks for the `primary purpose`, but the learner selects an option describing a possible benefit.",
      "**Action:** Identify task words and restate the question before comparing options.",
      "### 4. Application or priority error",
      "The learner knows the general content but applies it at the wrong stage or priority.",
      "**Example:** Selecting a treatment technique before addressing immediate safety or completing the necessary assessment.",
      "**Action:** Practice sequence, scope, and `first/next/best` reasoning.",
      "### 5. Confidence or guessing error",
      "The learner reaches the correct answer without a stable reason or holds an incorrect answer with excessive confidence.",
      "**Action:** Explain the principle before viewing the feedback and retest later with changed wording.",
      "### 6. Pacing or attention error",
      "The learner possesses the knowledge but rushes, overlooks a qualifier, or spends disproportionate time debating.",
      "**Action:** Use short timed sets, mark task words, and apply the item-decision process from OR-01.",
      "## The ExamPath error-log record",
      "The log should store the transferable lesson, not copyrighted or unnecessary question text.",
      "## Topic weakness versus reasoning weakness",
      "If all misses involve career theories, the learner may have a topic gap.",
      "If misses occur across ethics, assessment, and treatment planning whenever the stem asks what to do `first`, the learner may have a cross-topic priority-reasoning gap.",
      "The platform should therefore allow two simultaneous tags:",
      "- **What content was involved?**\n- **What thinking process failed?**",
      "## Suggested progress states",
      "The following states are ExamPath learning signals, not official pass predictions.",
      "### Priority",
      "Use when one or more of the following is present:",
      "- A repeated conceptual error\n- A high-confidence misconception\n- Consistently weak performance on a sufficiently sampled area\n- A clinically or ethically consequential sequence error",
      "### Developing",
      "Use when:",
      "- The learner understands the foundation but performance remains inconsistent.\n- The sample is growing but not yet stable.\n- Performance improves on focused items but not on mixed application.",
      "### Maintaining",
      "Use when:",
      "- The learner has answered multiple new items accurately.\n- Evidence comes from more than one date.\n- The learner can explain the distinction or rule.\n- At least some evidence comes from mixed or applied contexts.",
      "### Insufficient evidence",
      "Use when:",
      "- Too few new items have been attempted.\n- Most performance comes from repeated items.\n- The available questions do not represent the topic's important demands.",
      "The system should never label a topic `mastered forever.` Maintaining is more accurate because important material should continue to reappear.",
      "## Weekly error-log review",
      "Once each week, the learner should:",
      "1. Group errors by topic.\n2. Group errors by reasoning type.\n3. Identify repeated high-confidence errors.\n4. Select no more than three primary correction targets for the next week.\n5. Schedule delayed retests with new items.\n6. Move improving areas into maintenance only after varied evidence appears.",
      "Limiting primary targets prevents an error log from becoming an unmanageable list of everything the learner has ever missed.",
      "## Common exam-preparation traps",
      "- Recording only the topic and not the cause\n- Copying answer letters without a governing rule\n- Treating every miss as a knowledge deficit\n- Closing an error after one immediate correct retry\n- Focusing on percentage while ignoring high-confidence misconceptions\n- Allowing the error log to grow without scheduling follow-up",
      "## Related lessons",
      "- OR-02, Lesson 1 — Diagnostic Baseline and Readiness Profile\n- OR-02, Lesson 3 — Spaced Review and Retrieval Practice",
      "## Practice this topic",
      "- Classify the last five missed or uncertain questions by error type.\n- Select the two most frequent patterns and schedule a new-item retest.",
      "---"
    ],
    "keyTakeaways": [
      "Classify why an error occurred before choosing the remedy.",
      "Track both content and reasoning patterns.",
      "High-confidence misconceptions and repeated priority errors deserve early attention.",
      "Use new, delayed items to decide whether a correction is stable.",
      "A strong area moves into maintenance; it does not disappear from the plan."
    ],
